timeline.body:
I think the NPE with this query


```
FROM employees
| INLINESTATS max_salary = MAX(salary) by languages 
| KEEP emp_no, languages, max_salary
```

is due to [PhysicalPlanOptimizer.ProjectAwayColumns](https://github.com/elastic/elasticsearch/blob/main/x-pack/plugin/esql/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/optimizer/PhysicalPlanOptimizer.java#L122-L127).
In this case, `rhs` and `lhs` contain the same fields, so removing one of the two we also remove the other one (that we'll need later in the planning).

We could fix it by just checking that we are not removing something we still need, eg.

```
if (p instanceof HashJoinExec join) {
    attributes.removeAll(join.addedFields());
    for (Attribute rhs : join.rightFields()) {
        if (join.leftFields().stream().anyMatch(x -> x.semanticEquals(rhs)) == false) {
            attributes.remove(rhs);
        }
    }
}
```

I tried this against `nyc_taxis` and it's working pretty well. I tried writing a fairly small INLINESTATS.
```
while time curl -HContent-Type:application/json -XPOST -uelastic:password localhost:9200/_query?pretty -d'{
    "profile": true,
    "query": "FROM nyc_taxis | INLINESTATS a=AVG(fare_amount) BY passenger_count | WHERE fare_amount > a | STATS COUNT(*)"
}' | tee out; do echo -n ; done
```

That takes about a second.  If you take the times from the output:
```
jq '[.profile.drivers[].operators[] | {
        operator,
        nanos: (.status.processing_nanos + .status.process_nanos + .status.aggregation_nanos + .status.hash_nanos),
    }] | sort_by(.nanos)' out
```

You get it spending most of the time performing the lookup part of the join:
```
  {
    "operator": "RowInTableLookup[PackedValuesBlockHash{groups=[0:INT], entries=10, size=864b}, keys=[passenger_count], mapping=[1]]",
    "nanos": 896830117
  },
  {
    "operator": "RowInTableLookup[PackedValuesBlockHash{groups=[0:INT], entries=10, size=864b}, keys=[passenger_count], mapping=[1]]",
    "nanos": 1006208435
  },
  {
    "operator": "RowInTableLookup[PackedValuesBlockHash{groups=[0:INT], entries=10, size=864b}, keys=[passenger_count], mapping=[1]]",
    "nanos": 1165030794
  },
  {
    "operator": "RowInTableLookup[PackedValuesBlockHash{groups=[0:INT], entries=10, size=864b}, keys=[passenger_count], mapping=[1]]",
    "nanos": 1173068625
  }
```

If you make it intentionally worse like so:
```
 while time curl -HContent-Type:application/json -XPOST -uelastic:password localhost:9200/_query?pretty -d'{
    "profile": true,
    "query": "FROM nyc_taxis | INLINESTATS a=AVG(fare_amount) BY passenger_count, total_amount | WHERE fare_amount > a | STATS COUNT(*)"
```

It takes about 10 seconds. There are a lot of `total_amount`s. Here's the runtimes:
```
  {
    "operator": "RowInTableLookup[PackedValuesBlockHash{groups=[0:INT, 1:DOUBLE], entries=80473, size=3.9mb}, keys=[passenger_count, total_amount], mapping=[1, 2]]",
    "nanos": 3059240033
  },
  {
    "operator": "HashAggregationOperator[blockHash=PackedValuesBlockHash{groups=[1:INT, 2:DOUBLE], entries=48908, size=2.1mb}, aggregators=[GroupingAggregator[aggregatorFunction=SumDoubleGroupingAggregatorFunction[channels=[3]], mode=INITIAL], GroupingAggregator[aggregatorFunction=CountGroupingAggregatorFunction[channels=[3]], mode=INITIAL]]]",
    "nanos": 3069974199
  },
  {
    "operator": "HashAggregationOperator[blockHash=PackedValuesBlockHash{groups=[1:INT, 2:DOUBLE], entries=49142, size=2.1mb}, aggregators=[GroupingAggregator[aggregatorFunction=SumDoubleGroupingAggregatorFunction[channels=[3]], mode=INITIAL], GroupingAggregator[aggregatorFunction=CountGroupingAggregatorFunction[channels=[3]], mode=INITIAL]]]",
    "nanos": 3104720977
  },
  {
    "operator": "RowInTableLookup[PackedValuesBlockHash{groups=[0:INT, 1:DOUBLE], entries=80473, size=3.9mb}, keys=[passenger_count, total_amount], mapping=[1, 2]]",
    "nanos": 3232667218
  },
  {
    "operator": "RowInTableLookup[PackedValuesBlockHash{groups=[0:INT, 1:DOUBLE], entries=80473, size=3.9mb}, keys=[passenger_count, total_amount], mapping=[1, 2]]",
    "nanos": 3521781424
  }
```

Like in the other case we're spending most of the time looking up positions for the implicit JOIN, though the hash agg itself takes about the same amount of time. That's pretty expected, really.

Here's another fun one:
```
while time curl -HContent-Type:application/json -XPOST -uelastic:password localhost:9200/_query?pretty -d'{
    "profile": true,
    "query": "FROM nyc_taxis | EVAL d = DATE_TRUNC(1 day, pickup_datetime) | INLINESTATS a=AVG(fare_amount) BY d | WHERE fare_amount > a | STATS COUNT(*) BY d"
